00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:58.220
Selling cash secured puts at higher deltas than the common 20 delta recommendation lets investors collect premium while improving the odds of actually acquiring shares they want to own.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:15.680
The approach treats the premium as compensation for waiting, similar to placing a limit order that pays you until it fills. In today's environment of tariff relief and sector rotation into Canadian resources, the strategy suits names with solid balance sheets where you would happily take delivery.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:31.760
Implementation starts by scanning the options chain on a platform like interactive brokers or quest trade for 30 to 45 delta puts on stocks trading above key moving averages, then sizing the position so the cash reserve covers 100 shares per contract.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:41.660
The tactic has historically performed best in sideways or mildly bullish markets where implied volatility remains elevated but fundamentals support ownership.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:53.060
The main risk is opportunity cost if the stock rallies sharply before assignment, always verify the underlying meets your fundamental criteria first, rather than chasing yield alone.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:04.500
A 20 delta put expires worthless 80% of the time, which means an investor chasing only premium will miss owning the stock on the majority of occasions when the price moves favorably.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:13.660
Higher delta puts raise the probability of assignment while still generating income, aligning the trade with actual ownership goals instead of pure yield hunting.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:25.780
Platforms such as quest trade display the full options chain with delta, volume and open interest so users can compare 30 delta versus 20 delta contracts side by side before placing the order.

