00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:57.660
Imagine you hold a broad Canadian equity ETF and notice longer dated yields dropping after the treasury doubles its debt buyback cap to at least $4 billion per operation starting September 9.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:05.300
The mechanism works through reduced net supply of longer maturity bonds which lifts their prices and compresses yields.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:12.440
Lower yields then support higher equity multiples because the discount rate applied to future cash flows declines.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:25.000
With the announcement time between refunding cycles and just hours before a $16 billion 20 year sale the move signals officials responded to market pressure rather than following a preset schedule.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:35.340
Professionals always check the treasury operation calendar and the size of each buyback relative to the $2 billion prior cap before assuming the yield effect will persist.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:42.040
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating every debt management announcement as neutral background noise.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:52.660
The single sentence fix is to map the buyback size and timing directly against the 10 year yield reaction before adjusting duration exposure in your fixed income sleeve.

